2026-09-10T07:01Z claude note author asked whether the parity tickets reach feature parity with #6012, naming the startup wait as an example. Verified at the pinned head 19e7962, still the PR head today (draft, open, updated 2026-09-02): embeddings.js init() delays 5 s before startIndexing() so it does not compete with window setup. The sitter has no equivalent -- it awaits initializationPromise and uiReadyPromise, then arms the sweep at setTimeout 0 -- and no ticket, SPEC section or DECISIONS entry names a boot-window grace. The suites do not assert it either, so the suite-driven matrix could not have found it: exit criterion added for a source-read arm, and the sitter row routed to 0756.
2026-09-11T14:38Z claude note verified against 6012's sdtTest.js at 19e7962: ensure() blocks and regenerates on a stale-processor pack, not only a missing one. Installed build's document-worker/metadata.json read directly: SDT_SCHEMA_VERSION 1.1.0->1.2.0, PDF processor 3->14 across three point releases (10.0 build 0817 to 10.0.2 build 0909), unannounced by either upstream repo. RELEASE-NOTES.md's eagerness-is-durable claim corrected: the cost is one-time only between processor bumps, and 0755's stale-processor exit criterion covers classification correctness, not this recurrence/frequency claim -- no row currently owns the release-notes framing
2026-09-13T18:37Z claude note THE THREE-POINT-RELEASE INTERVAL IS ONE RELEASE, measured rather than bounded. This ticket's 2026-09-11 note, SYNC.md's Zotero-extraction row and plugins/sdt-sitter/RELEASE-NOTES.md all record the SDT constants moving across THREE point releases between 10.0 (build 20260817151751) and 10.0.2 (build 20260909184950), and RELEASE-NOTES.md adds that whether the delta was one jump or several is NOT KNOWABLE FROM HERE. It is knowable, and without installing anything: the three constants are a static JSON at resource/document-worker/metadata.json inside each release's app/omni.ja (a zip), so a release tarball answers it with no profile, no data directory and no running Zotero. Read from all three release tarballs plus padme's installed build with verification/probes/sdt_stamps_by_release.py (added here): 10.0 / 20260817151751 = schema 1.1.0, pack 1, pdf 3, epub 1, snapshot 1; 10.0.1 / 20260824184709 = IDENTICAL on every constant; 10.0.2 / 20260909184950 = schema 1.2.0, pack 1, pdf 14, epub 2, snapshot 1. THE WHOLE DELTA IS THE SINGLE STEP 10.0.1 -> 10.0.2, and 10.0 -> 10.0.1 moved nothing. TWO CONTROLS ARE BUILT IN: padme's installed 10.0.1 and the separately fetched 10.0.1 tarball agree field for field, so reading a tarball equals reading an install; and 10.0.0 is NOT a Zotero release -- the download endpoint answers 403 for it on every platform and both channels while 10.0, 10.0.1 and 10.0.2 answer 206 -- so the probe demonstrably distinguishes absent from unreadable. CONSEQUENCE, and it cuts against eagerness harder than the current RELEASE-NOTES text: the interval between the processor bumps that invalidate the pack cache is ONE point release, not three, so eager preparation's one-time cost recurs about three times more often than three-point-releases-were-enough implies. SUPERSEDED BY THIS ENTRY: this ticket's 2026-09-11 note, SYNC.md's across the three point releases, and RELEASE-NOTES.md's not-knowable-from-here plus its three point releases were enough to move it. Those two documents are NOT edited here -- per this ticket's own 2026-09-11 note no row owns the release-notes framing, and both files are contended shared metadata.
2026-09-13T18:37Z claude note WHAT A RUNTIME VERSION MATRIX WOULD COST, since the stamp question above needed none. Only two Zotero 10 releases can be run side by side, not three: 10.0.0 does not exist and 10.0.3 is unreleased, so the roster is 10.0, 10.0.1 and 10.0.2 (10.0.2 is current latest). The Linux build is a self-contained tar.xz needing no root, so N extracted directories coexist trivially; the constraints are all runtime. ONE, the data directory must be per version and never shared: schema.js gates a downgrade on a manually-incremented compatibility version (padme reads userdata 129, compatibility 9 against this build's _maxCompatibility 9), so an older client refuses a newer store only when a migration bumped compatibility and OTHERWISE OPENS IT and operates on a schema it does not know -- silent, not refused. Pin with -datadir ABSOLUTE (dataDirectory.js requires an absolute path or the literal profile, and the parent must exist); note 0782's finding that the dataDir PREF alone is inert without useDataDir, which is why the command line is the safer pin here. TWO, extensions.zotero.httpServer.port defaults to 23119 for every instance, so two running clients collide on the local API -- the surface this repository drives -- and the second fails to bind; give each profile its own port or run one at a time. THREE, auto-update silently un-pins a matrix arm: application.ini carries an AppUpdate URL and the install tree has updates/, so each test profile needs app.update disabled. Concurrency also needs -no-remote, and the shipped launcher already exports MOZ_ALLOW_DOWNGRADE=1 so Gecko's own profile-downgrade guard is off.
2026-09-17T08:09Z MinhHaDuong note rebaseline v1.20.2 (c386e83): upstream's withdrawal of the #6012 meanVector citation (3a1e942, v1.17.0) is partial. It removed the citation from MEAN_SAMPLE's comment (sqlite-index.ts:216-222) as unsourceable (no meanVector anywhere in zotero/zotero); the same claim still stands on packCode()'s docstring at sqlite-index.ts:2298-2302. No row of verification/6012-GUARANTEE-PARITY.md exists yet (first exit criterion unchecked), so no committed matrix leans on either; SPEC.md's calibration passage (around :2150) now says the mean centering is zoteus's own mechanism and names the surviving citation. When the matrix is written, flag both citations and do not source mean centering to #6012
